1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,090
Imagine it's 1970,

2
00:00:02,410 --> 00:00:04,590
and you hide a $100 bill under

3
00:00:04,390 --> 00:00:05,010
your mattress.

4
00:00:05,643 --> 00:00:07,083
You forget about it for 56

5
00:00:06,883 --> 00:00:07,563
years,

6
00:00:07,843 --> 00:00:09,043
and find it again today.

7
00:00:09,563 --> 00:00:10,423
It's still $100.

8
00:00:11,043 --> 00:00:13,023
But now it only buys about

9
00:00:12,823 --> 00:00:14,703
as much as $12 did back

10
00:00:14,503 --> 00:00:15,003
then.

11
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:16,885
So where did the other $88

12
00:00:16,685 --> 00:00:17,405
go?

13
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:22,850
The answer is inflation:

14
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:23,950
the slow,

15
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:26,090
steady rise of prices across the

16
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:26,730
whole economy.

17
00:00:27,241 --> 00:00:27,981
To measure it,

18
00:00:27,901 --> 00:00:29,701
the government tracks a huge basket

19
00:00:29,501 --> 00:00:30,741
of things people buy:

20
00:00:31,341 --> 00:00:31,541
food,

21
00:00:31,801 --> 00:00:32,201
rent,

22
00:00:32,521 --> 00:00:32,901
fuel,

23
00:00:33,061 --> 00:00:33,781
clothes,

24
00:00:33,781 --> 00:00:34,421
transport,

25
00:00:34,481 --> 00:00:35,261
and doctor visits.

26
00:00:35,875 --> 00:00:36,535
Every month,

27
00:00:36,375 --> 00:00:38,235
it records the prices of about

28
00:00:38,035 --> 00:00:39,475
80,000 items,

29
00:00:39,275 --> 00:00:41,435
in thousands of stores,

30
00:00:41,455 --> 00:00:42,355
rental homes,

31
00:00:42,155 --> 00:00:43,695
and doctors' offices.

32
00:00:44,275 --> 00:00:45,075
Put it all together,

33
00:00:44,875 --> 00:00:46,355
and you get one number:

34
00:00:46,615 --> 00:00:47,935
the Consumer Price Index.

35
00:00:49,317 --> 00:00:50,397
Let's make that real.

36
00:00:50,957 --> 00:00:52,737
Here's what some everyday things cost

37
00:00:52,537 --> 00:00:53,577
in 1980,

38
00:00:53,937 --> 00:00:55,697
according to government price surveys,

39
00:00:56,037 --> 00:00:57,277
and what they cost now.

40
00:00:57,738 --> 00:00:59,138
A pound of white bread went

41
00:00:58,938 --> 00:01:01,538
from 51 cents to $1.83.

42
00:01:02,338 --> 00:01:03,358
A dozen eggs,

43
00:01:03,158 --> 00:01:06,498
from 84 cents to $2.27.

44
00:01:07,138 --> 00:01:08,158
A pound of coffee,

45
00:01:07,958 --> 00:01:12,138
from $3.14 to $9.33.

46
00:01:12,998 --> 00:01:13,998
And a gallon of gas,

47
00:01:13,798 --> 00:01:18,098
from $1.25 to $4.48.

48
00:01:18,874 --> 00:01:20,874
Different things rise at different speeds.

49
00:01:21,354 --> 00:01:23,174
But the direction is almost always

50
00:01:22,974 --> 00:01:23,674
the same:

51
00:01:23,954 --> 00:01:24,334
up.

52
00:01:25,583 --> 00:01:27,123
Here's the full index for the

53
00:01:26,923 --> 00:01:28,403
United States since 1950.

54
00:01:29,483 --> 00:01:30,103
Notice something?

55
00:01:30,323 --> 00:01:31,843
It almost never goes down.

56
00:01:32,362 --> 00:01:34,682
Something that cost $1 in 1970

57
00:01:34,482 --> 00:01:38,182
costs about $8.66 today.

58
00:01:38,650 --> 00:01:41,310
That's prices rising by almost 4%

59
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:41,630
a year,

60
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:42,510
on average,

61
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,010
for more than half a century.

62
00:01:45,283 --> 00:01:46,963
So why do prices keep climbing?

63
00:01:47,443 --> 00:01:48,443
The simplest answer:

64
00:01:49,023 --> 00:01:50,703
when more money chases the same

65
00:01:50,503 --> 00:01:51,443
amount of stuff,

66
00:01:51,463 --> 00:01:52,443
prices go up.

67
00:01:52,873 --> 00:01:55,873
Economists call one version demand-pull inflation.

68
00:01:56,473 --> 00:01:56,913
People,

69
00:01:57,053 --> 00:01:57,593
businesses,

70
00:01:57,853 --> 00:01:59,473
and governments try to spend more

71
00:01:59,273 --> 00:02:00,733
than the economy can produce,

72
00:02:01,153 --> 00:02:02,853
often when borrowing is cheap,

73
00:02:03,193 --> 00:02:04,513
so sellers raise prices.

74
00:02:05,177 --> 00:02:06,757
The other is cost-push inflation.

75
00:02:07,557 --> 00:02:09,697
When oil spikes or factories shut

76
00:02:09,497 --> 00:02:10,137
down,

77
00:02:10,217 --> 00:02:11,737
it costs more to make things,

78
00:02:11,997 --> 00:02:13,717
and those costs get passed on

79
00:02:13,517 --> 00:02:13,997
to you.

80
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:16,070
Most of the time,

81
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:17,930
inflation is slow and quiet.

82
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:19,070
But sometimes,

83
00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:19,750
it explodes.

84
00:02:20,414 --> 00:02:21,574
In March 1980,

85
00:02:21,814 --> 00:02:24,494
prices were rising almost 15% a

86
00:02:24,294 --> 00:02:24,714
year.

87
00:02:25,294 --> 00:02:26,734
And in June 2022,

88
00:02:27,114 --> 00:02:28,294
after the pandemic,

89
00:02:28,094 --> 00:02:30,294
inflation hit about 9%,

90
00:02:30,094 --> 00:02:32,094
the highest in four decades.

91
00:02:33,483 --> 00:02:35,183
When inflation gets out of hand,

92
00:02:35,323 --> 00:02:37,263
central banks fight back with one

93
00:02:37,063 --> 00:02:37,883
main weapon:

94
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:38,983
interest rates.

95
00:02:39,515 --> 00:02:41,795
Higher rates make borrowing more expensive.

96
00:02:42,475 --> 00:02:44,555
People and businesses borrow less and

97
00:02:44,355 --> 00:02:45,095
spend less,

98
00:02:45,435 --> 00:02:46,655
demand cools down,

99
00:02:46,915 --> 00:02:48,495
and prices calm down with it.

100
00:02:49,617 --> 00:02:50,537
In 1981,

101
00:02:51,017 --> 00:02:52,777
to crush double-digit inflation,

102
00:02:53,377 --> 00:02:55,137
the Federal Reserve pushed its key

103
00:02:54,937 --> 00:02:57,277
interest rate to about 19%.

104
00:02:57,077 --> 00:02:58,357
It worked,

105
00:02:58,657 --> 00:03:00,357
but it also helped trigger a

106
00:03:00,157 --> 00:03:01,117
painful recession.

107
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:02,710
In 2022,

108
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:04,990
it raised rates from almost zero

109
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:07,030
to over 5% in about a

110
00:03:06,830 --> 00:03:07,730
year and a half,

111
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:09,930
the fastest climb in four decades.

112
00:03:11,283 --> 00:03:12,643
So shouldn't the goal be zero

113
00:03:12,443 --> 00:03:12,943
inflation?

114
00:03:14,023 --> 00:03:14,223
Surprisingly,

115
00:03:14,643 --> 00:03:14,983
no.

116
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:17,383
The US Federal Reserve aims for

117
00:03:17,183 --> 00:03:18,243
about 2%.

118
00:03:18,745 --> 00:03:20,645
Because falling prices can be even

119
00:03:20,445 --> 00:03:20,905
worse.

120
00:03:21,525 --> 00:03:22,785
If you expect things to be

121
00:03:22,585 --> 00:03:23,705
cheaper next month,

122
00:03:23,765 --> 00:03:24,205
you wait.

123
00:03:24,905 --> 00:03:25,905
Businesses sell less,

124
00:03:26,065 --> 00:03:26,705
cut jobs,

125
00:03:26,885 --> 00:03:28,425
and prices fall even further.

126
00:03:29,001 --> 00:03:31,061
A little inflation keeps money moving.

127
00:03:32,317 --> 00:03:34,337
But inflation doesn't treat everyone the

128
00:03:34,137 --> 00:03:34,557
same.

129
00:03:35,063 --> 00:03:36,303
If you keep your savings in

130
00:03:36,103 --> 00:03:36,523
cash,

131
00:03:36,843 --> 00:03:37,263
you lose.

132
00:03:37,803 --> 00:03:39,743
Your money quietly buys less every

133
00:03:39,543 --> 00:03:40,383
single year.

134
00:03:40,797 --> 00:03:41,957
If you owe money at a

135
00:03:41,757 --> 00:03:42,797
fixed interest rate,

136
00:03:43,097 --> 00:03:44,197
you can actually win,

137
00:03:43,997 --> 00:03:45,737
because you pay it back with

138
00:03:45,537 --> 00:03:46,937
dollars that are worth less.

139
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:49,090
And if your pay rises slower

140
00:03:48,890 --> 00:03:49,630
than prices,

141
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:51,210
you're getting a pay cut,

142
00:03:51,430 --> 00:03:53,310
even if your salary never changes.

143
00:03:54,683 --> 00:03:56,283
And that last one matters more

144
00:03:56,083 --> 00:03:56,963
than you might think.

145
00:03:57,430 --> 00:03:58,270
In 1970,

146
00:03:58,650 --> 00:04:00,910
a typical non-manager worker in America

147
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:03,870
earned about $3.40 an hour.

148
00:04:04,630 --> 00:04:04,830
Today,

149
00:04:05,030 --> 00:04:07,210
it's about $32.60.

150
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:09,670
That looks like a massive raise.

151
00:04:10,331 --> 00:04:11,551
But adjust for inflation,

152
00:04:11,351 --> 00:04:13,111
and the picture changes.

153
00:04:13,871 --> 00:04:14,951
In today's dollars,

154
00:04:15,091 --> 00:04:17,411
that worker's hourly pay is only

155
00:04:17,211 --> 00:04:19,371
about 4% higher than it was

156
00:04:19,171 --> 00:04:20,331
in 1973.

157
00:04:21,099 --> 00:04:22,519
Most of the raise was eaten

158
00:04:22,319 --> 00:04:23,659
by rising prices.

159
00:04:24,379 --> 00:04:26,299
This doesn't count benefits like health

160
00:04:26,099 --> 00:04:26,619
insurance,

161
00:04:27,059 --> 00:04:28,279
but the lesson stands:

162
00:04:28,639 --> 00:04:30,319
what matters isn't how much you

163
00:04:30,119 --> 00:04:30,519
earn.

164
00:04:30,819 --> 00:04:32,119
It's what your pay can buy.

165
00:04:33,383 --> 00:04:34,463
Here's a handy trick.

166
00:04:35,183 --> 00:04:37,083
Divide 70 by the inflation rate,

167
00:04:37,263 --> 00:04:38,723
and you get roughly how many

168
00:04:38,523 --> 00:04:40,243
years it takes for prices to

169
00:04:40,043 --> 00:04:40,423
double.

170
00:04:40,909 --> 00:04:41,769
At 2%,

171
00:04:41,569 --> 00:04:44,009
prices double in about 35 years.

172
00:04:44,509 --> 00:04:45,329
At 7%,

173
00:04:45,129 --> 00:04:46,149
in just 10.

174
00:04:47,383 --> 00:04:49,243
That's why many people don't leave

175
00:04:49,043 --> 00:04:51,183
long-term savings sitting in cash.

176
00:04:51,923 --> 00:04:53,543
They look for places where money

177
00:04:53,343 --> 00:04:54,843
can grow at least as fast

178
00:04:54,643 --> 00:04:55,403
as prices,

179
00:04:55,903 --> 00:04:57,923
knowing that every option comes with

180
00:04:57,723 --> 00:04:58,443
its own risks.

181
00:04:59,026 --> 00:05:00,966
This channel isn't financial advice.

182
00:05:01,446 --> 00:05:02,626
But the lesson is simple:

183
00:05:02,846 --> 00:05:04,826
money that sits still slowly melts.

184
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:06,550
So,

185
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:07,750
where did your $100 go?

186
00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:08,630
Nowhere.

187
00:05:08,990 --> 00:05:09,710
It just shrank,

188
00:05:09,510 --> 00:05:10,810
a few cents at a time.

189
00:05:11,286 --> 00:05:12,706
If this changed how you see

190
00:05:12,506 --> 00:05:13,126
your money,

191
00:05:13,186 --> 00:05:15,386
subscribe to Coin Theory.

192
00:05:15,186 --> 00:05:15,866
Next time:

193
00:05:16,026 --> 00:05:17,766
why houses got so expensive.

